{"name":"Bow tie","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man_%2824217739102%29.jpg/960px-Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man_%2824217739102%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man_(24217739102).jpg","caption":"Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man (24217739102)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A bow tie is a small knotted band of fabric worn at the collar. It sits right under the jaw, so it draws the eye up toward the face and neck and reads as a deliberate, polished style choice.","It traces to the cravat tradition of the 17th to 19th centuries and is the classic piece for black-tie and white-tie evening wear. It also has a reputation as a distinctive or quirky accent in everyday dress."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Wearers choose between self-tie (the traditional strip tied by hand), pre-tied and clip-on versions, and shapes such as bat wing, butterfly and diamond point. Self-tied versions are the usual choice for formal evening wear."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No health risks. Fit of the collar and the size of the bow relative to the face and neck affect how it looks, and commentators note it can carry associations with conservatism or nerdiness depending on context."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man_%2824217739102%29.jpg/960px-Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man_%2824217739102%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man_%2824217739102%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Bow-tie-businessman-fashion-man (24217739102)","license":"CC0